.docname {Development}
.include {docs}

## Everyday commands

```sh
cargo test                  # run the test suite
cargo fmt                   # apply formatting
cargo fmt -- --check        # check formatting
cargo clippy -- -D warnings # lint, warnings as errors
cargo clean                 # remove build artifacts
```

## Tests

| Location | Covers |
|----------|--------|
| `src/lib.rs`, `tests` module | Deterministic randomness, TOML parsing, probability validation, edge movement, combat, procreation, starvation, phased turn order |
| `src/main.rs` | CLI argument parsing |
| `tests/seeded_e2e_snapshots.rs` | End-to-end runs against a fixed config and seed |

The end-to-end test builds a throwaway `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` under `target/` and
seeds it from `simulation.example.toml`, so it never touches your real
configuration file.

## Benchmarks

.repolink {benches/board_refresh.rs} {blob/main/benches/board_refresh.rs} uses
Criterion to measure `Board::refresh` on a 25×25 board and a 100×100 board with
800 creatures per species, over single and multi-turn runs, from seed `42`.

```sh
cargo bench
```

Criterion writes HTML reports to `target/criterion`.

CI runs each benchmark once, untimed, with `cargo test --bench '*'`, so a
benchmark that stops working is caught. It does not compare timings. To check
a change for speed, run `cargo bench` before and after it on the same machine.

## Continuous integration

GitHub Actions is configured in
[`.github/workflows/ci.yml`](https://github.com/andreashdez/creature_life_cycle/blob/main/.github/workflows/ci.yml). The
workflow runs on pushes to `main` and on pull requests. Its Linux job installs
the libraries Bevy needs to compile, then runs:

```sh
cargo fmt --check
cargo clippy --locked --all-targets -- -D warnings
cargo test --locked
cargo test --locked --bench '*'
cargo check --locked --no-default-features --all-targets
RUSTDOCFLAGS="-D warnings" cargo doc --locked --no-deps --document-private-items
```

The fourth check runs each benchmark once, untimed, in Criterion's test mode, so
a benchmark that compiles but panics is caught; `cargo test` alone does not run
bench targets. Timings are not checked in CI, because shared runners vary too
much between runs to catch a real slowdown. The fifth check keeps the `gui`
feature gate honest: the library, CLI, benches, and tests must still build
without Bevy. The last one builds the API docs,
private items included, and fails on warnings such as broken doc links.

A second job runs `cargo test --locked` on macOS. The GUI ships as a macOS app,
and Bevy compiles different windowing and rendering code there, so a Linux-only
build would miss breakage on the platform that is actually released.

> Tip: Running those six commands locally before pushing reproduces the Linux
> job exactly.

[Dependabot](https://github.com/andreashdez/creature_life_cycle/blob/main/.github/dependabot.yml)
opens weekly pull requests for Cargo dependencies and GitHub Actions. Patch
releases are grouped into one pull request; larger updates, such as a new Bevy
or `rand_chacha`, get their own. CI runs on each, so an update that changes
seeded runs fails the snapshot tests rather than merging quietly.

## Packaging

```sh
sh scripts/package_gui.sh
```

The script refuses to run outside macOS, builds `--release`, assembles
`target/package/Creature Life Cycle.app`, and zips it to
`target/package/creature_life_cycle_gui-macos.zip`. The version in `Info.plist`
comes from `cargo pkgid`, so bumping `Cargo.toml` is enough.

## Working on this documentation site

The site is written in [Quarkdown](https://quarkdown.com) and lives in `docs/`.

.filetree
    - docs
      - _setup.qd
      - _nav.qd
      - **main.qd**
      - getting-started.qd
      - cli.qd
      - gui.qd
      - configuration.qd
      - simulation-rules.qd
      - architecture.qd
      - development.qd
      - assets/

`_setup.qd` holds the theme and shared helpers, `_nav.qd` defines the sidebar,
and `main.qd` is the entry point. Every other page is a subdocument reached
through the navigation.

Everything in `docs/` is site source. Internal engineering notes live in
`notes/` instead, as plain Markdown, so that they are not compiled into the
site; see [Architecture](architecture.qd#engineering-notes).

Build the site:

```sh
cd docs
quarkdown c main.qd --strict
```

Preview it with live reload while editing:

```sh
cd docs
quarkdown c main.qd -p -w
```

> Note: A page is only compiled if something links to it. When you add a page,
> add it to `_nav.qd` as well, or it stays an orphan and is skipped.

### Publishing

[`.github/workflows/docs.yml`](https://github.com/andreashdez/creature_life_cycle/blob/main/.github/workflows/docs.yml)
builds the site and deploys it to GitHub Pages on every push to `main` that
touches `docs/`. The workflow pins the Quarkdown version it builds with, caches
that toolchain, and builds with `--strict`, so an unresolved function call
fails the run rather than publishing a degraded page.

Pull requests build the site but do not deploy it, which catches breakage
before it reaches `main`.

Bumping the Quarkdown version is a one-line change to `QUARKDOWN_VERSION` in
the workflow. Do it deliberately: a new release can change the rendered
output.
